ad on a  television it was quite straight forward  he wasn't posturing or anything he said  you know sometimes the televisions more  important than food Banerjee also likes  to pepper his lectures with personal  anecdotes  he tells students that development aid  is a process limited to small steps  he says relief programs always need to  be monitored and updated  he's even advising the United Nations to  revise their list of Development Goals  benarjee says it's far too long what  they've come up with this idea 169  targets I think is self-defeating it's  just nobody's going to say if you go and  tell a country well you have to meet 169  goals they'll say we don't ever have the  time to measure them let alone achieve  them it's just I'm going to happen so I  think somewhere it has to the process  has to give and we have to go down to  like 30 or something or or I think this  whole thing is going to become pointless  his lectures are popular with the  students they're innovative in he gets  his point across you can tell he's  really interested in the subject he's  encouraged me to take a new approach to  these issues and be more precise about  the data and have a more objective  perspective  various philosophies are shared even  during the break and they told us to buy  things that were made in our country  Banerjee's time in germany has come to  an end but the discussion on how to  fight world poverty is long from over
 

